Denoising as Projection: Constrained Optimization with Gradient-Guided Diffusion Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license arXiv:2608.29507v1 [cs.LG] 30 Aug 2026 Denoising as Projection: Constrained Optimization with Gradient-Guided Diffusion Runyu Zhang Jiawei Zhang Gioele Zardini Affiliation: MIT, {runyuzha,gzardini,amins,asuman}@mit.edu Saurabh Amin Affiliation: MIT, {runyuzha,gzardini,amins,asuman}@mit.edu Asuman Ozdaglar † † thanks: * corresponding author Affiliation: University of Wisconsin–Madison, jzhang2924@wisc.edu Abstract Diffusion models are increasingly used not only for sampling from learned data distributions, but also for generating samples that optimize task-specific objectives. A common approach is to guide the reverse diffusion process using gradients of an external objective. However, when the data distribution is supported on a structured feasible set, such as a manifold or a constraint set, gradient guidance can move samples away from the learned data geometry. In this paper, we study a simple projected-gradient-guided diffusion update based on the observation that the Stein denoising operator can act as an approximate projection onto the data geometry. The proposed update incorporates the objective gradient inside the denoising step, yielding an inference-time method that uses only a pretrained denoiser and gradient evaluations. We analyze this update as an inexact projected-gradient method for constrained optimization over learned feasible geometries. Our theory covers three settings: linear manifolds, compact convex feasible sets, and compact Riemannian submanifolds. In all these settings, we prove descent and finite-time convergence guarantees. Numerical experiments support the theoretical interpretation and illustrate how the proposed update balances objective descent with preservation of the learned geometry. 1 Introduction Diffusion models have emerged as a powerful class of generative models, achieving state-of-the-art performance in image synthesis, inverse problems, scientific design, and decision-making tasks [ 35 , 14 , 39 , 36 ] . Their success stems in large part from score-based denoising dynamics, which can model complex high-dimensional data distributions. In many applications, however, the goal is not merely to sample realistic data, but to generate samples that are also good with respect to a task-specific objective. Examples include inverse problems [ 5 ] , trajectory optimization [ 16 ] , molecular generation [ 43 ] , control [ 3 ] , and other settings where samples should remain consistent with a learned data distribution while achieving low cost or high reward. A common strategy for steering diffusion models toward desirable samples is guidance. Classifier guidance and classifier-free guidance modify the reverse diffusion dynamics using auxiliary information so that generated samples better satisfy a target condition [ 10 , 15 ] . More generally, when a differentiable objective function f : ℝ n → ℝ f:\mathbb{R}^{n}	o\mathbb{R} is available, one can guide sampling by incorporating the objective gradient into the reverse dynamics. This suggests viewing guided diffusion as a method for solving the constrained optimization problem min x ∈ 𝒳 ⁡ f ⁡ ( x ) , \min_{x\in\mathcal{X}}f(x), where the feasible set 𝒳 ⊆ ℝ n \mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n} is implicitly represented by the support or high-density region of a learned data distribution. This perspective is particularly relevant when the diffusion model serves as a generative prior for feasible designs, trajectories, or decisions, and the objective encodes a task-specific performance criterion. Gradient guidance is attractive in this setting because it can be applied at inference time and requires only evaluations of ∇ f 
abla f . Recent work has begun to study guided diffusion from an optimization perspective [ 11 ] . A key difficulty, however, is that directly injecting an gradient into the reverse dynamics may disrupt the feasibility structure encoded by the generative model. This difficulty is fundamentally geometric. Depending on the application, 𝒳 \mathcal{X} may be a low-dimensional data manifold, a closed convex constraint set, a set of physically admissible designs, or a family of dynamically feasible and collision-free trajectories. An arbitrary gradient descent direction need not be tangent to a manifold, feasible for a constrained region, or compatible with the learned data geometry. Consequently, naive gradient guidance may reduce the objective value while moving the iterate away from 𝒳 \mathcal{X} , thereby degrading sample quality, physical validity, or constraint satisfaction. This issue is particularly important in applications such as diffusion-based robotic planning, where the learned trajectory distribution captures useful behavioral and dynamical priors, but objective guidance can produce infeasible or unsafe trajectories unless an additional feasibility mechanism is introduced [ 44 , 34 , 24 , 25 ] . Several lines of work have addressed this issue by modifying the guidance or sampling procedure, but often at the cost of additional sample, computational or modeling complexity. For example, Kharitenko et al. [19] constructs explicit geometric primitives that rely on computing the Jacobian of the score function, which can be more computationally costly; Guo et al. [11] rely on repeated batch generation and objective linearization. Closely related denoiser-as-projection analysis [ 22 ] establishes recovery guarantees for linear inverse problems with a quadratic data-fitting objective under an RIP-type condition, rather than general constrained optimization. Beyond these, manifold-aware guidance [ 7 , 13 , 6 , 45 ] , constrained-diffusion methods [ 4 , 23 , 18 , 12 ] , and reward-alignment approaches [ 8 , 9 , 31 , 29 , 20 , 1 ] address related problems, but may rely on reward-specific fine-tuning, repeated sampling and auxiliary models; detailed comparisons are deferred to Appendix A . These existing approaches raise a natural question: can a much simpler update (without outer-loop generation, score-Jacobian evaluation, or model fine-tuning), using only one gradient evaluation and one pretrained Stein denoising call per step, already provide optimization guarantees? We answer this question by leveraging the fact that the Stein denoising operator can approximate projection onto the learned data geometry. This suggests a natural way to incorporate objective information: we apply the denoiser after the gradient update. Intuitively, the gradient step promotes descent of the objective, while the subsequent denoising step pulls the iterate back toward the feasible geometry. When the denoiser is close to the true projection, the resulting update behaves like an inexact projected-gradient method. Contributions. First, we introduce Denoising-Corrected Gradient (DCG) Guidance, a simple inference-time method that applies an objective-gradient step followed by one call to a pretrained denoiser at each reverse diffusion step. DCG operates along a single reverse trajectory and requires neither repeated batch generation or score-model adaptation [ 11 ] , nor differentiation of the denoiser [ 19 ] . Second, we show that the Stein posterior-mean denoiser can act as the geometric correction operator underlying DCG. We establish quantitative approximation bounds between the denoiser and the relevant projection operator for linear Gaussian, compact convex sets, and compact smooth submanifolds settings. These results allow us to interpret the reverse diffusion dynamics as a time-varying inexact projected-gradient method. Table 1 : Comparison of theoretical scope and inference-time requirements. Sublinear rates are best-iterate guarantees; constants and finite-noise or denoising-error terms are omitted. Method Geometry Guarantee Rate Score requirement Sample requirement [ 11 ] Linear–Gaussian Regularized optimality of the distributional mean Geometric Jacobian of score function Multiple reverse-diffusion trajectories [ 19 ] Compact C 3 C^{3} Riemannian manifold Riemannian stationarity O ( T − 1 / 2 ) O(T^{-1/2}) Jacobian of score function No auxiliary inference-time samples DCG (ours) Linear–Gaussian Global optimality; strongly convex f f Geometric Don’t require Jacobian Single reverse trajectory; no auxiliary samples Compact convex set Global optimality; strongly convex f f Geometric Projected stationarity; smooth nonconvex f f O ( T − 1 / 2 ) O(T^{-1/2}) Compact C 2 C^{2} Riemannian manifold Riemannian stationarity; smooth nonconvex f f O ( T − 1 / 2 ) O(T^{-1/2}) Third, we derive finite-time convergence guarantees that reflect the geometry and objective class. For linear-Gaussian data and strongly convex objectives, both the projected objective error and the normal component contracts geometrically. For compact convex feasible sets, we establish geometric convergence for strongly convex objectives, and an O ⁡ ( 1 / T ) O(1/\sqrt{T}) best-iterate bound for the nonconvex objectives. Finally, for compact smooth submanifolds, we obtain an O ⁡ ( 1 / T ) O(1/\sqrt{T}) best-iterate bound for local convergence. Thus, the analysis covers linear subspaces, possibly nonsmooth convex feasible sets, and smooth manifolds within a unified approximate-projection framework. Table 1 summarizes the theoretical distinction from the closest optimization-oriented approaches. Finally, we provide numerical experiments (in Appendix B ) on synthetic constrained optimization, motion planning, and standard diffusion-based reinforcement learning benchmarks from D4RL. Across these settings, DCG achieves a consistently favorable tradeoff between objective value and constraint satisfaction, outperforming standard gradient guidance and matching or improving upon closely related optimization-oriented diffusion baselines. Limitations. Our analysis assumes an exact Stein posterior-mean denoiser and that the learned data support faithfully represents the feasible set. Learned-score errors can be incorporated through additional projection-error terms, but a full treatment of model misspecification is left for future work. We also focus on deterministic DDIM dynamics under a variance-exploding diffusion with an exponentially decaying noise schedule. Extending the theory to stochastic samplers, other diffusion parameterizations, and more general noise schedules requires tracking the corresponding time-varying relaxation and denoising errors. 2 Preliminaries Constrained optimization over learned data geometry. We consider the constrained optimization problem min x ∈ 𝒳 ⁡ f ⁡ ( x ) , \displaystyle	extstyle\min_{x\in\mathcal{X}}f(x), (1) where f : ℝ n → ℝ f:\mathbb{R}^{n}	o\mathbb{R} is a differentiable objective and 𝒳 ⊆ ℝ n \mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n} denotes the feasible geometry represented by the data distribution. Depending on the application, 𝒳 \mathcal{X} may be a linear subspace, a compact convex set, or a smooth embedded submanifold. We assume that a diffusion model has been trained on samples supported on, or concentrated near, 𝒳 \mathcal{X} , and study how the pretrained model can be used at inference time to approximately solve equation 1 . If the Euclidean projector π 𝒳 \pi_{\mathcal{X}} were available, a natural optimization method would be projected gradient descent, x + = π 𝒳 ( x − η ∇ f ( x ) ) . \displaystyle x^{+}=\pi_{\mathcal{X}} igl(x-\eta
